  "text": "2008B09 What is\nhumidity and how can it be measured?\nList:\n·\nHumidity\n·\nNormal values\n·\nHygrometer types\nHumidity:\nDefinitions\n·\nHumidity: an index of the water vapour content of a gas mixture\n·\nAbsolute humidity: is amount of water / volume of air\n·\nRelative humidity: is actual partial pressure / saturated vapour\n  pressure (at that temp)\nImportance\n(no marks for this last time)\n·\nPrevent water loss (by evaporation)\n·\nPrevent heat loss (latent heat of vapourisation)\n·\nPrevent crusting of secretion\n·\nMaintain mucociliary function hence sterility\nNormal values:\nTemperature (\n°\nC)\nAbsolute (g/m\n3\n)\nRelative\nRoom air\n20\n7\n40%\nNasopharynx\n30\n30\n95%\nLungs\n37\n43\n100%\nHygrometers:\nHair hygrometer\n·\nHair under constant tension\n·\nHair is hygroscopic = absorbs moisture\n·\n↑ humidity -> ↑ hair length -> movement of dial\n·\nMost accurate between 20-90% relative humidity\n·\nPros: simple and cheap\n·\nCons: slow response, difficult to couple to electrical circuit\nWet and dry bulb hygrometer\n·\nDry thermometer measures ambient temp\n·\nWet thermometer surrounded by a wick\n·\nEvaporation causes cooling (latent heat)\n·\nAmbient temperature and wet-dry temp difference are used to\n  estimate relative humidity from a reference table\n·\nAccurate between 30-90% relative humidity\n·\nPros: simple and cheap\n·\nCons: slow response, evaporation accelerated if airflow\nRegnault’s hygrometer\n·\nSilver tube for high thermal conductivity\n·\nBubbling air evaporates ether\n·\nEther temp falls\n·\nDew point: temp at which relative humidity of surrounding air is\n  100% and condensation occurs on tube\n·\nSVP for different temperatures is known in tables.\n·\nRelative humidity = actual vapour pressure / SVP ambient = SVP dew\n  point / SVP ambient\n·\nTables used to correlate temperature, absolute and relative\n  humidity\n·\nPros: simple and cheap\n·\nCons: ether pollution\nElectrical hygrometers\n·\nMultiple devices\n·\ne.g. resistance, capacitance, electronic dewpoint\n·\nMost measure both absolute and relative humidity\nFeedback welcome at\nketaminenightmares@gmail.com",
